  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $f:X\\to Y$ and $g:Y\\to Z$. Prove the following:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $g\\circ f$ is injective, then $f$ is injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $f(x)=f(y)$, then $(g\\circ f)(x)=(g\\circ f)(y)$. Injectivity of $g\\circ f$ gives $x=y$, so $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Let $x,y\\in X$ and suppose $f(x)=f(y)$. Applying $g$ gives\n$$g(f(x))=g(f(y)).$$\nSince $g\\circ f$ is injective, $x=y$. Thus $f(x)=f(y)$ implies $x=y$, so $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $g\\circ f$ is injective, then $g$ need not be injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Take $X=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$, $Y=Z=\\mathbb Z$, $f(n)=n$ and $g(n)=n^2$.\n\nThe composite is injective on positive integers, but $g(1)=g(-1)$, so $g$ is not injective.",
          "worked": "Let $X=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$ and $Y=Z=\\mathbb Z$. Define $f:X\\to Y$ by $f(n)=n$ and $g:Y\\to Z$ by $g(n)=n^2$. Then\n$$(g\\circ f)(n)=n^2.$$\nFor $m,n\\in X$, equality $(g\\circ f)(m)=(g\\circ f)(n)$ gives $m^2=n^2$. Since $m,n>0$, $m=n$. Hence $g\\circ f$ is injective.\n\nHowever, $g(1)=1=g(-1)$ while $1\\ne-1$. Therefore, $g$ is not injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      }
    ]
  },
